---
import LearnArticle from "../../../components/LearnArticle.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from "../../../components/FAQ.astro";

const intro =
  "Everyone asks whether the lump sum or the annuity costs less in tax. Let's stop guessing and work it through: a hypothetical $100 million cash win, single filer, 2026 brackets — both paths, same assumptions, every figure labeled an estimate.";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: "In the example, which pays less tax: lump sum or annuity?",
    answer:
      "At this scale the federal marginal rate is nearly identical either way — even a single $6.67 million annuity payment lands mostly in the 37% top bracket. The real differences come from timing: when income is recognized, when withholding happens, and the time value of money. This article shows the mechanics; it doesn't recommend either path.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why is the annuity total bigger than the cash value in the example?",
    answer:
      "The $200 million advertised jackpot is the nominal total of 30 payments spread over 29 years; the $100 million cash value is what that stream is worth today. The gap is the time value of money, not a bonus — a dollar in year 29 buys less than a dollar today.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does the annuity really save on taxes?",
    answer:
      "Less than people assume, at jackpot scale. The often-quoted 'annuity tax advantage' matters most for smaller wins, where spreading income can keep payments in lower brackets. On $100 million, nearly every dollar is in the top bracket either way — the annuity's edge is timing and discipline, not a lower rate.",
  },
  {
    question: "What assumptions does the worked example use?",
    answer:
      "A hypothetical $100M cash value ($200M advertised jackpot), single filer, 2026 federal brackets, 0% annual payment growth (equal payments — Powerball publishes no fixed graduation rate, so this is an assumption), no state tax shown, no other income. Change any of them and the numbers move; the <a href=\"/lottery-payout-calculator/\" class=\"font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline\">payout calculator</a> lets you do exactly that.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I choose lump sum or annuity based on this?",
    answer:
      "No article can answer that for you. Taxes are one input among many — investment returns, spending discipline, estate planning, and your personal situation matter at least as much. Talk to a fiduciary financial adviser before claiming.",
  },
];

const sourceIds = [
  "irs-rev-proc-2025-32",
  "irs-gambling-withholding",
  "powerball-official-rules",
];
---

<LearnArticle
  slug="/learn/annuity-vs-lump-sum-tax-example/"
  intro={intro}
  faqs={faqs}
  sourceIds={sourceIds}
>
  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">The setup: one hypothetical win, two paths</h2>
  <p>
    Our hypothetical winner takes home a <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">$100
    million cash value</strong> — the lump sum. The advertised jackpot behind
    it is <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">$200 million</strong>,
    the nominal total of 30 annuity payments over 29 years. (That 2:1 ratio
    is illustrative, not a rule — the real ratio moves with interest rates,
    which is why you always enter the actual published cash value.) Single
    filer, 2026 federal brackets, no state tax in this example, no other
    income. <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">Every dollar figure
    below is an estimate.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Path A: the lump sum, all in one year</h2>
  <p>
    Take the cash and the entire $100 million is taxable income in a single
    year. The federal brackets are marginal, but at this scale marginal
    barely matters: after the standard deduction, nearly the whole $100
    million falls in the 37% top bracket. Rough estimate:{" "}
    <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">~$37 million federal</strong>,
    before the standard deduction shaves a little off.
  </p>
  <p>
    At payout, the lottery withholds 24% — $24 million — as a prepayment.
    Come filing time, the bill is ~$37 million, the credit is $24 million,
    and our winner owes roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">$13
    million more</strong>, plus whatever their state charges. That's the
    withholding-vs-liability gap in action: the 24% was never the bill.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Path B: the annuity, $6.67 million a year</h2>
  <p>
    Take the annuity and the $200 million arrives as 30 payments. With our
    0% growth assumption — equal payments, since Powerball publishes no
    fixed graduation rate — each annual payment is about{" "}
    <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">$6.67 million</strong>,
    taxed as ordinary income in the year it's received.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here's the part most articles skip: a $6.67 million payment still lands
    mostly in the 37% top bracket. So the federal <em>rate</em> on each
    payment is nearly the same as the lump sum's rate. Each year gets its
    own standard deduction and its own trip through the lower brackets —
    worth something, but a small something next to $6.67 million. Rough
    estimate per year: ~$2.4 million federal per payment, ~$72 million
    nominal total across 30 years — <em>nominal</em> dollars, spread over
    three decades.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Where the difference actually comes from</h2>
  <p>
    If the tax rates are nearly identical, what's really different? Three
    things:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">Timing of income.</strong>{" "}
      The lump sum concentrates everything into one tax year; the annuity
      spreads recognition across 30. At this scale the bracket benefit is
      small — but for smaller wins, spreading income can genuinely keep
      payments in lower brackets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">Time value of money.</strong>{" "}
      $72 million paid over 29 years is worth far less than $72 million
      today. Comparing nominal totals flatters the annuity; the honest
      comparison discounts later payments.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-800">Withholding and cash flow.</strong>{" "}
      The lump sum hands $24 million to the IRS on day one and demands ~$13
      million more at filing. The annuity withholds a slice of each payment,
      year by year — no single-year shock, but three decades of tax returns
      to file.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">What this example can't tell you</h2>
  <p>
    Real life adds state tax (zero in some states, significant in others),
    your other income, and what you'd actually <em>do</em> with a lump sum —
    invest it, spend it, or watch it evaporate. None of that fits in an
    article, and none of it points to a universal answer. Run your own
    numbers in the{" "}
    <a href="/powerball-payout-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      Powerball payout calculator
    </a>{" "}
    or the{" "}
    <a href="/lottery-payout-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      generic payout calculator
    </a>
    , read the{" "}
    <a href="/learn/cash-option-vs-annuity/" class="font-semibold text-blue-700 hover:underline">
      cash-vs-annuity guide
    </a>
    , and then talk to a fiduciary adviser. The math can be estimated; the
    decision can't be outsourced.
  </p>
</LearnArticle>
